cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Value-Field Theory (VFT)"
message: "If you use this theory or repository, please cite this repository."
authors:
  - family-names: "Nuno"
    given-names: "T."
    affiliation: "Independent Researcher"
abstract: >
  Value-Field Theory (VFT) is a structural framework for describing economic and
  organizational activity from resource state K, actor-specific resource state K_i,
  unrealized-relation credit or reference state P_i, actor-side activity A_i, and
  action-generating field configurations. Institutional categories such as firms,
  states, markets, money, and capital are treated primarily as derived structures or
  projections rather than primitive variables. P_i is latent: its true internal
  structure and dimensionality are not fixed by the Core, while observable proxies may
  be used to estimate it and scalar approximations may be used in financial or economic
  projections. Use-value is treated as the physical realization of a resource through
  use or consumption rather than as a universal utility scalar. Surplus is represented
  resource-specifically as available K beyond required K and, when repeatedly generated,
  can create degrees of freedom for future activity. Money is treated as a mechanism
  that generalizes and preserves exchangeability, while accounting values and capital
  valuations are projection-specific representations rather than Core primitives.
  VFT also proposes three non-reducible Core behavioral hypotheses for recurrent
  management and decision processes: resource-realization, activity-flow, and
  P-downside. These hypotheses are not ontological definition conditions and need not
  be reduced to a single objective function. Institutional actors may also influence
  P directly through actions such as guarantees, sanctions, certification, policy,
  ranking, or platform control before corresponding resource changes are realized.
repository-code: "https://github.com/nuno929/vf-model"
